  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 229 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nA shortwave trough is forecast to lift from the southern Rockies\non Saturday and into the Texas and Oklahoma panhandles by Saturday\nevening. An associated cold front will push through our forecast\narea late Saturday afternoon into Saturday night. The winds will\ninitially shift westerly to northwesterly over western portions of\nour CWA, with a much drier airmass moving in with dewpoints dropping\ninto the upper 20s to mid 30s. A plume of low level moisture with\ndewpoints in the upper 40s to mid 50s will be in place over the\neastern Permian Basin and Lower Trans Pecos where a few showers\ncould develop Saturday morning. Expect winds to turn northwesterly\nto northerly and increase in speed following the passage of the\ncold front Saturday evening/night. Saturday still looks to be a\nfairly warm day with highs in the upper 60s to upper 70s over most\nlocations. Temperatures on Saturday night will be colder with lows\nfalling into the mid to upper 30s over parts of southeast New\nMexico, the northwest Basin, and the higher terrain, while\nreadings fall into the lower 40s to lower 50s over the remainder\nof the region. Surface high pressure builds into our forecast area\non Sunday and highs will trend cooler in the mid 50s to lower 60s\nover all but far southern portions of our region. Temperatures\ntrend slightly colder on Sunday night and could fall into the\nlower to mid 30s over our northern zones and higher terrain areas. \n\nA dry zonal (westerly) flow pattern aloft is expected on Monday.\nCool surface high pressure will continue to build into much of\nsoutheast New Mexico and west Texas, so high temperatures should\nremain slightly below average with readings in the 50s and 60s.\nEnsemble guidance members generally show the next upper-level\ntrough translating from the Rocky Mountains into the central and\nsouthern Plains Monday into Tuesday. A few showers could develop\nover portions of the area ahead of this system Monday and Monday\nnight but rain chances are currently very low (10-20%). A \nreinforcing surface ridge/cold front may build back into our \nregion Tuesday and Tuesday night, which may keep temperatures \nbelow normal. Highs are currently forecast to range in the lower \n50s to lower 60s for most on Tuesday. Lows on Tuesday night are \nforecast to drop into the lower 30s to lower 40s, with potential \nfor a light freeze over our far northern zones and the higher \nterrain areas. The GFS and ECMWF ensembles generally keep zonal \nflow intact through next Wednesday with dry conditions expected \nbefore a potential system amplifies by late next week. For now \nPOPs remain too low to mention going into Wednesday night and \nThanksgiving Day given very low confidence in how this next system\nmay or may not develop. It currently appears that temperatures \nwill continue to remain below normal through the middle to latter \npart of next week.",
